data.content:
Here are the three C# 13 breaking changes and their fixes:

---

**1. `Buffer.cs` — `[InlineArray]` on record structs is now prohibited**

C# 13 disallows `[InlineArray]` on `record struct` types (they synthesize methods incompatible with the single-field requirement). Fix: use a plain `struct`.

```csharp
[System.Runtime.CompilerServices.InlineArray(10)]
struct Buffer
{
    private int _element0;
}
```

---

**2. `Generator.cs` — `stackalloc` in local functions within iterators is now disallowed**

C# 13 extended `ref struct` support into iterators/async methods but simultaneously tightened safety rules: `stackalloc` is no longer allowed in local functions captured by an iterator. Fix: extract it to a regular method.

```csharp
unsafe class DataGenerator
{
    IEnumerable<int> Generate()
    {
        yield return 1;
        Process();
    }

    static void Process()
    {
        int* p = stackalloc int[10];
        // ... work with pointer
    }
}
```

---

**3. `Resolver.cs` — Overload resolution ambiguity with collection expressions**

C# 13 improved `Span`/`ReadOnlySpan` support in overload resolution, making `[]` now match both overloads equally. What was unambiguous in C# 12 is now ambiguous. Fix: add an explicit cast.

```csharp
class Resolver
{
    static void Process(ReadOnlySpan<int> ros) {}
    static void Process(Span<object> s) {}

    static void Main()
    {
        Process((ReadOnlySpan<int>)[]);
    }
}
```

---

**Summary**

| File | Breaking change | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| `Buffer.cs` | `[InlineArray]` banned on records | `record struct` → `struct` |
| `Generator.cs` | `stackalloc` banned in iterator local fns | Extract to a class method |
| `Resolver.cs` | Span overload ambiguity with `[]` | Add explicit cast |
